#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110337 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110564 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b903c40f-a2e5-5084-9c10-b2d33f423f1e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "9662033b-2a3a-5d80-91ad-ed542898b80b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14544" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 6^2)$ with $P(X > 4) = 0.1841,$ find $\\mu.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.00 \\,$ | $\\, 0.01 \\,$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7580 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7611 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7642 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7881 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7939 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8159 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8186 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8212 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =1-P(X>4) \\\\  & =1-0.1841 \\\\  & =0.8159\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =0.8159 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{4-\\mu }{6}) & =0.8159 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4-\\mu }{6}) & =0.8159 \\\\ \\frac{4-\\mu }{6} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8159)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.90) = 0.8159 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.8159) = 0.90.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4-\\mu }{6} & =0.90 \\\\ 4-\\mu  & =5.4 \\\\ \\mu  & =-1.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110338 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110565 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "765f5ee4-b683-5c14-8e99-32241c029f39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/id #uuid "35cc4c4c-79fb-5ae2-94e0-79f007af832b" :question/math-academy-id "e-14545" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X\\sim N(2.5, \\sigma^2)$ and $P(X \\geq 3) = 0.4013,$ find $\\sigma^2.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.2 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5910 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5948 \\,$ | $\\, 0.5987 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.3 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6293 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6331 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6368 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6736 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X\\ge 3) \\\\  & =1-0.4013 \\\\  & =0.5987\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =0.5987 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3-2.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5987 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma }) & =0.5987 \\\\ \\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.5987)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.25) = 0.5987 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.5987) = 0.25.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.5}{\\sigma } & =0.25 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{0.5}{0.25} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2}={2}^{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110339 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110566 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2bd470f-5459-5860-94cd-4ce9e6b70642" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Zero"}] :question/id #uuid "2a8633b4-f937-57e3-899d-c3176f07a48f" :question/math-academy-id "e-14546" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(0, \\sigma^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P(-3.18 <        X <        3.18) = 0.4038,$ find $\\sigma.$\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.02 \\,$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.4 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6628 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6664 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6700 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.6985 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7019 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7054 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7324 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7357 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7389 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-3.18<X<3.18) & =0.4038 \\\\ 2P(0<X<3.18) & =0.4038 \\\\ P(0<X<3.18) & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18)-P(X<0) & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18)-0.5 & =0.2019 \\\\ P(X<3.18) & =0.7019 \\\\ P(X\\le 3.18) & =0.7019\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3.18) & =0.7019 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{3.18-0}{\\sigma }) & =0.7019 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{3.18}{\\sigma }) & =0.7019 \\\\ \\frac{3.18}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7019)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.53) = 0.7019 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.7019) = 0.53.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3.18}{\\sigma } & =0.53 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{3.18}{0.53} \\\\ \\sigma  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110340 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero"}] :question/id #uuid "5eb435ff-ef12-5f18-89d6-4ac163fae344" :question/math-academy-id "e-14548" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\\Phi(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X \\sim N(\\mu, 2^2)$ is a normal random variable with $P( -4\\mu<             X <        6\\mu) = 0.6528,$ find $\\mu$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.03 \\,$ | $\\, 0.04 \\,$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7673 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7704 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.8 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7967 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7995 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8023 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.9 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8238 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8264 \\,$ | $\\, 0.8289 \\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-4\\mu<             X <        6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu.$*" :question/worked-solution "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -5\\mu <     X <     \\mu +5\\mu) = 0.6528,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-4\\mu<             X <       6\\mu$ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu.$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -5\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.6528 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.6528 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu )-0.5 & =0.3264 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +5\\mu ) & =0.8264 \\\\ P(X\\le 6\\mu ) & =0.8264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 6\\mu ) & =0.8264 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{6\\mu -\\mu }{2}) & =0.8264 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{5\\mu }{2}) & =0.8264 \\\\ \\frac{5\\mu }{2} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.8264)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.94) = 0.8264 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad \\Phi^{-1}( 0.8264) = 0.94.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5\\mu }{2} & =0.94 \\\\ 5\\mu  & =1.88 \\\\ \\mu  & ≈0.38\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110565 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "765f5ee4-b683-5c14-8e99-32241c029f39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Variance or Standard Deviation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66a8e441-b6b2-591b-944b-3cfddb0ac398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315690}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66a8e441-b6b2-591b-944b-3cfddb0ac398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315690} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2534596-9656-56a2-a31c-8adf543ca897" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4a054739-7d5f-59e4-b190-1f466818f897" :question/math-academy-id "q-341655" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(3,{\\sigma }^{2})$ and $P(X\\ge 1)=0.8413,$ use the table to find $\\sigma .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n\n$\\sigma =$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert the given probability into a cumulative probability using the complement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X<1) \\\\  & =1-P(X\\ge 1) \\\\  & =1-0.8413 \\\\  & =0.1587\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =0.1587 \\\\ P\\,(Z\\le \\frac{1-3}{\\sigma }) & =0.1587 \\\\ Φ\\,(-\\frac{2}{\\sigma }) & =0.1587 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.00)=0.1587\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.1587)=-1.00.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sigma } & =-1 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{2}{1} \\\\ \\sigma  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110567 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "977d4d41-250e-525a-8a6e-754014eeef3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110264} {:answer/id #uuid "a3ae23e6-f0fa-5a7f-b00a-f9abc6ea2b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110265} {:answer/id #uuid "aca32477-9181-5710-81a8-b5e5ea9f087c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110266} {:answer/id #uuid "161ff13b-589f-5910-ada4-8e14b71bf343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110267} {:answer/id #uuid "7f84f079-9f75-5ea1-bd4c-f022d59f04c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110268}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b96a4158-7099-5762-a920-d57e9cd07c5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186110263}] :question/id #uuid "02f6c3de-17f2-58af-afa6-5936538a62e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-94875" :question/problem "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(μ, 3^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-0.7μ < X < 2.7μ) = 0.3256$, find $μ$ rounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n| $z$ | $0.01$ | $0.02$ | $0.03$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $0.3$ | $0.6217$ | $0.6255$ | $0.6293$ |\n| $0.4$ | $0.6591$ | $0.6628$ | $0.6664$ |\n| $0.5$ | $0.6950$ | $0.6985$ | $0.7019$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-0.7μ < X < 2.7μ$ is symmetric about the mean $μ$.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
